WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.999 --> 00:00:03.860
We have a lot of choices
to make about our diet.

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:05.980
Add to that, doing
the right thing

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:07.160
when it comes
to preventing

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:09.170
and treating a
chronic disease,

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:11.719
fighting a virus,
or losing weight,

00:00:11.719 --> 00:00:13.509
and suddenly our
nutrition choices

00:00:13.509 --> 00:00:15.750
can seem almost
overwhelming.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:18.529
Well, I’m here to help.

00:00:18.529 --> 00:00:20.400
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.990
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:28.920
Today we begin a two-part series
on the effects of fasting on cancer.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:30.400
Do we feed cancer?

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:31.920
Do we starve it?

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:34.329
What do randomized trials tell us?

00:00:34.329 --> 00:00:37.110
Here’s our first story.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:40.289
In 1974, an influential paper
was published decrying

00:00:40.289 --> 00:00:45.270
physician-induced malnutrition as
the skeleton in the hospital closet ---

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:48.530
the fact that many patients in
hospitals were malnourished,

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:51.170
which the editorial board
of the journal of the AMA

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:53.079
described as shocking.

00:00:53.079 --> 00:00:56.219
"Even a single case
is one too many,"

00:00:56.219 --> 00:00:59.570
yet still to this day
the issue persists.

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:02.670
If anything, people with serious
illness would seem to need

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:06.000
even more nutrition,
not less, yet underfeeding

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.550
persists, involving as many as
50% of hospitalized patients.

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:14.799
The ethical principle of justice
requires that every patient

00:01:14.799 --> 00:01:17.350
be fed enough, given
that hospital malnutrition

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:19.040
has been associated
with increased

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:24.240
risk of disease and death,
but is it cause and effect?

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:27.250
Does eating less
make you sicker,

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:31.100
or does being sicker
just make you eat less?

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:35.240
You don't know until
you put it to the test.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:38.900
But would it be ethical to randomize
patients to remain starved?

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:42.710
I mean wouldn't nutritional
support obviously help?

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:44.840
It turns out, no.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:47.720
Not one,
but 22 randomized controlled

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:50.159
trials involving thousands
of malnourished patients

00:01:50.159 --> 00:01:52.610
found that, sure,
you can plump them up;

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:56.439
however, there seemed to be
little effect on clinical outcomes.

00:01:56.439 --> 00:02:00.159
In fact, sometimes it can
actually make things worse.

00:02:00.159 --> 00:02:05.180
Maybe your body is losing
your appetite on purpose.

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:09.209
Ever since Hippocrates, fasting
has been offered as a treatment

00:02:09.209 --> 00:02:12.620
for acute and chronic diseases,
based on the observation

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:15.819
that when people get sick, they
frequently lose their appetite,

00:02:15.819 --> 00:02:21.050
so maybe that's part of our body's
wisdom and we shouldn't force it?

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:24.480
OK, but that was 2400 years ago.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:27.160
What have we learned since?

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:30.420
Along with fever, decreased
food consumption is indeed

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:33.290
one of the most common signs
of infection, often regarded

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:37.099
as an undesirable
manifestation of sickness,

00:02:37.099 --> 00:02:40.879
but it's actually an active,
beneficial defense mechanism.

00:02:40.879 --> 00:02:44.409
Now obviously, chronic
under-nutrition can impair

00:02:44.409 --> 00:02:47.670
our defenses, but data
suggest that in the short-term,

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:51.620
immune function can be enhanced
by lowering food intake.

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:57.159
Some of the data are crazy,
like 95% alive versus 95% dead

00:02:57.159 --> 00:03:00.100
after the same infection,

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:03.360
but that was in mice
starved for 48 hours.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:07.520
Obviously, you can't randomize
people to a fatal infection,

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.379
but what they showed is that
the blood from starved mice was

00:03:10.379 --> 00:03:13.540
nearly 8 times better at
killing off the invading bacteria

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:15.030
in a petri dish.

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:16.700
It dramatically boosted

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:19.980
the capacity of their white blood
cells to kill off the pathogens.

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:23.650
Why can't we just test people
like that?

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:26.060
Indeed, we can.

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:28.099
Researchers fasted
people for two weeks

00:03:28.099 --> 00:03:30.439
on an 80 calorie a day diet,

00:03:30.439 --> 00:03:33.160
and their white blood cells
showed the same kind of boost

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:37.770
in bacteria-killing activity,
a boost in antibody production,

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:42.980
and natural killer cell activity
increased by an average of 24%.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:46.220
Now that's especially interesting
because our natural killer cells

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:51.959
don't just help clear infections,
but also kill cancer cells.

00:03:51.959 --> 00:03:55.379
In fact, that's how they measured
natural killer cell activity by pitting

00:03:55.379 --> 00:04:01.750
them against K562 cells --- those are
tumor cells, human leukemia cells.

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:04.689
So two weeks of fasting boosted
their bloodstream's ability

00:04:04.689 --> 00:04:07.810
to kill off cancer cells by 24%.

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:10.950
So fasting is said to improve
anticancer immunosurveillance,

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:12.650
or more poetically

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:15.910
"stimulate the appetite of
the immune system for cancer.

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:16.910
"

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:19.959
So why isn't fasting used more
to treat cancer?

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:21.049
Until recently,

00:04:21.049 --> 00:04:23.400
fasting therapy was not considered
to be a treatment option

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:25.720
in cancer, related to the fact that
a common therapeutic goal

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:28.720
in palliative cancer treatment
is to avoid weight loss

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:32.360
and to counteract the wasting
syndrome known as cachexia,

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:36.890
which is the ultimate cause
of death in many cancer cases.

00:04:36.890 --> 00:04:39.780
Tumors are voracious,
rapidly expanding,

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:41.810
and needing lots of
energy and protein,

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:45.350
and so metabolically
reprograms our body

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:48.870
to start breaking down to feed it.

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:52.870
It does this by triggering
inflammation throughout the body.

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:55.250
It's not just that people
lose their appetite.

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:57.350
The fundamental difference
between weight loss

00:04:57.350 --> 00:05:01.040
observed in cancer cachexia
and that seen in simple starvation

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:05.370
is the lack of reversibility
with feeding alone.

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:08.500
For example, here's the weight of
a cancer patient that started to drop.

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:11.450
No wonder: they were only taking
in a few hundred calories a day.

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:14.509
So in addition to giving them
about 100 grams of protein a day,

00:05:14.509 --> 00:05:20.180
they stuck a tube into a vein and
infused up to 4,000 calories a day.

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:21.300
But it didn't matter.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:24.840
They continued to lose weight.

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:27.540
Therapeutic nutritional
interventions to correct or reverse

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:30.000
cachexia have met
with little success.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.840
The best treatment for
cancer cachexia therefore

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:37.280
is to treat the cause
and cure the cancer.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:40.330
In fact, maybe forcing extra
nutrition on cancer patients

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:44.110
could be playing right
into the tumor's hands.

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:47.100
Like in pregnancy when the
fetus gets first dibs on nutrients

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:48.759
even at the mother's expense,

00:05:48.759 --> 00:05:53.020
the tumor may be first
in the feeding line.

00:05:53.020 --> 00:05:54.920
Maybe our loss of appetite
when we get cancer

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:57.550
is even a protective response.

00:05:57.550 --> 00:06:02.860
But in the 1960s, TPN was born ---
total parenteral nutrition ---

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:04.990
where people no
longer had to eat;

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:07.729
and you could infuse all the
nutrition people needed straight

00:06:07.729 --> 00:06:12.730
into their veins, and the modern
era of nutrition support was born.

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:15.860
It became widely accepted
and implemented,

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:19.360
growing into a
multibillion-dollar industry.

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:23.870
So should it be routinely given to
malnourished cancer patients?

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:27.479
The answer is not as
obvious as one might think.

00:06:27.479 --> 00:06:30.550
When it was put to the test
in dozens of randomized trials,

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:33.740
the results were both
disappointing and surprising.

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:37.729
Parenteral nutrition didn't just
fail to provide any benefit

00:06:37.729 --> 00:06:40.780
to these patients;
it caused harm.

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:44.169
Not only did it appear to
provide zero survival benefit,

00:06:44.169 --> 00:06:47.900
there was an increase in
complications and infections,

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:52.070
and a decrease in tumor
response to chemotherapy,

00:06:52.070 --> 00:06:54.370
presumed to be due to all
those extra nutrients

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:56.490
stimulating tumor growth.

00:06:56.490 --> 00:06:58.229
Similarly, oral nutritional

00:06:58.229 --> 00:07:00.440
interventions in malnourished
patients with cancer,

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:05.400
like giving them bottles of Ensure,
found no survival advantage.

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:09.599
Despite the lack of demonstrated
benefit, the knee jerk reaction

00:07:09.599 --> 00:07:12.580
of many oncologists to the idea
of cancer patients fasting

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:15.720
is the concern they're not
eating enough already.

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:20.560
But you don't know until you
until you put it to the test,

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:23.090
which we'll explore next.

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:29.020
For the past 50 years, chemotherapy
has been a major medical treatment

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:31.169
for a wide range of cancers.

00:07:31.169 --> 00:07:34.760
Its main strategy has been largely
based on targeting cancer cells,

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:37.389
by means of DNA damage
caused in part by the

00:07:37.389 --> 00:07:39.050
production of free radicals.

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:41.371
Although these drugs
were first believed to be

00:07:41.371 --> 00:07:43.540
quite selective for tumor cells,

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:46.620
we now know that normal cells
also experience severe

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:51.220
chemotherapy-dependent damage,
leading to dose-limiting side effects

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:54.080
including bone marrow and
immune system suppression,

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:58.090
fatigue, vomiting, diarrhea,
and in some cases even death.

00:07:58.090 --> 00:08:01.250
And if you do survive, the DNA
damage to normal cells

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:04.570
can even lead to new
cancers down the road.

00:08:04.570 --> 00:08:07.270
There are cell-protecting
drugs that have been tried

00:08:07.270 --> 00:08:08.270
to reduce the side effects,

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:10.680
so you can pump in
higher chemo doses,

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:13.710
but these drugs have not been
shown to increase survival,

00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:17.570
in part because they may also
be protecting the cancer cells.

00:08:17.570 --> 00:08:19.860
What about instead using fasting

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:23.090
for cellular protection
during cancer treatment?

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:24.860
Fasting may have
an unrecognized role

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:27.030
in cancer prevention and treatment.

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:29.789
Short-term fasting before and
immediately after chemotherapy

00:08:29.789 --> 00:08:33.390
may minimize side effects,
while at the same time

00:08:33.390 --> 00:08:37.700
may actually make cancer cells
more sensitive to treatment.

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:39.080
That's exciting.

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:43.339
During nutrient deprivation,
healthy cells switch from growth

00:08:43.339 --> 00:08:46.800
to maintenance and repair,
but tumor cells are unable

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:51.000
to slow down their unbridled growth
due to growth-promoting mutations

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:54.700
that led them to become
cancer cells in the first place.

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:58.610
This inability to adapt to starvation
may represent an important

00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:02.950
Achilles' heel for many
types of cancer cells.

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:05.440
As a consequence of these
differential responses

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:09.800
of healthy versus cancer
cells to short-term fasting,

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:12.360
chemotherapy causes more
DNA damage and cell suicide

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:16.940
in tumor cells, while potentially
leaving healthy cells unharmed.

00:09:16.940 --> 00:09:20.100
Thus, short-term fasting
may protect healthy cells

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:22.350
against the toxic properties
of chemotherapy

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:25.750
and render tumor cells
more sensitive,

00:09:25.750 --> 00:09:27.470
or at least that's the theory.

00:09:27.470 --> 00:09:30.100
Let's test it out.

00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:33.860
Tyrosine kinase inhibitors
are a group of chemo drugs

00:09:33.860 --> 00:09:36.890
that are now the mainstay of
treatment in many types of cancer.

00:09:36.890 --> 00:09:37.890
Let's see them in action.

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:38.890
These are petri dishes
filled with cancer cells that

00:09:38.890 --> 00:09:39.890
have been dyed pink.

00:09:39.890 --> 00:09:40.890
Two types of lung cancer cells
and one type of colorectal cancer.

00:09:40.890 --> 00:09:41.890
This is the before, and this the
after, exposed to the chemo drugs

00:09:41.890 --> 00:09:42.890
erlotinib, gefitinib, crizotinib,
and regorafenib.

00:09:42.890 --> 00:09:43.890
See how there's less pink?

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:44.890
That shows how many cancer
cells the chemo killed off.

00:09:44.890 --> 00:09:45.890
Starve the cells for
24 hours first, though,

00:09:45.890 --> 00:09:46.890
and the drugs work even better.

00:09:46.890 --> 00:09:47.890
Even the starvation alone without
the drugs killed off a bunch of them.

00:09:47.890 --> 00:09:48.890
The researchers conclude
that these TKI drugs that are

00:09:48.890 --> 00:09:49.890
commonly administrated for
treating solid tumors become

00:09:49.890 --> 00:09:50.890
potentiated in their activity
by 24 hours of starvation,

00:09:50.890 --> 00:09:51.890
but that's in a petri dish.

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:52.890
Similar results were found
for breast cancer and

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:53.890
a chemo drug called doxorubicin.

00:09:53.890 --> 00:09:54.890
Here's five days of unhindered growth
of breast cancer in a petri dish,

00:09:54.890 --> 00:09:55.890
from 150,000 cancer cells
to 800,000 in under a week.

00:09:55.890 --> 00:09:56.890
Dripping on a little chemo can
knock it down to around 600,000,

00:09:56.890 --> 00:09:57.890
but starve those cells
for 48 hours first,

00:09:57.890 --> 00:10:00.660
and the chemo can completely
keep them in check.

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:05.460
Even the short-term starvation
alone can restrain growth.

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:07.970
Short-term fasting raises the
possibility that you could get

00:10:07.970 --> 00:10:12.610
twice the effect for half the dose,
which may be particularly important

00:10:12.610 --> 00:10:16.750
for a drug as toxic as doxorubicin,
which causes heart failure

00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:20.670
in as many as 1 in 3 at higher doses.

00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:25.190
Again, though, these are just
in vitro studies in a petri dish

00:10:25.190 --> 00:10:28.750
Then researchers moved to mice
and found the same dual benefit:

00:10:28.750 --> 00:10:30.850
less toxic chemo,
yet at the same time

00:10:30.850 --> 00:10:33.130
more effective,
and not just by a little.

00:10:33.130 --> 00:10:37.139
A high enough chemo dose
to kill 100% in less than a week.

00:10:37.139 --> 00:10:42.290
But after being starved for 60 hours,
after the same dose, 100% survived.

00:10:42.290 --> 00:10:43.610
Ok, that's the toxicity.

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:44.610
What about efficacy?

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:45.610
Here's tumor growth without
chemo, growth with chemo,

00:10:45.610 --> 00:10:46.610
and with chemo after
short-term fasting,

00:10:46.610 --> 00:10:49.019
and this translated
into improved survival.

00:10:49.019 --> 00:10:52.410
Interestingly, fasting alone
appeared to work

00:10:52.410 --> 00:10:57.850
as well as the chemo,
and the same with radiation.

00:10:57.850 --> 00:11:01.190
Unbridled tumor growth knocked
down by radiation therapy,

00:11:01.190 --> 00:11:03.630
and even more so after
the combination of radiation

00:11:03.630 --> 00:11:05.520
and alternate-day fasting,

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:11.470
but alternate-day fasting alone
seemed to do as well as the radiation.

